				<macro >
					<flowline name='Main' description='' >
						<command class_type='comment' title='' comment='Read Me
==========
-

Run the simulation and see how the numeric data is converted from
simple 8-bit binary numbers to binary characters, longer values require
arrays of 8-bit binary characters to represent the number. 

It is important to note that a character array is exactly the same as
a regular 8-bit byte only there are more of them. Character arrays or
Strings or byte arrays are all exactly the same to the microcontroller.
Byte arrays and Strings have different meanings in Flowcode to aid the 
line between numeric and alphanumeric data.

Arrays are indexed from 0 through to (size - 1) so an array of size 12 
would be indexed from 0 - 11.

Bytes, Ints and Strings all use the same syntax to create and reference 
arrays. In the variable manager you can create Byte or Int arrays by 
adding [x] after the variable name where x is the number of variables
in the array.

Eg. ByteArray[2]

Arrays can then be indexed in calculations and you can use loops and 
index counters to increase the efficiency of your code.

ByteArray[0] = 23
ByteArray[idx] = 15

The maximum Array size on the PICmicro devices is 256 bytes.' ccode='' textshow='1' textmode='0' textarea='12,0,12,0' break='0' mark='0' disable='0' hide='0' customize='0' />
						<command class_type='calculation' title='Assign character variables with ASCII numbers' comment='' ccode='' textshow='1' textmode='0' textarea='36,0,36,0' break='0' mark='0' disable='0' hide='0' customize='0' >
